Hey, I'm working on an embedded C++ project for an automotive ECU and I've run into a really frustrating issue. We're seeing a slow, steady memory leak that only shows up after about 4-5 hours of continuous operation. It's not hitting a wall immediately, just gradually exhausting the available RAM. 

Here is a simplified snippet of the telemetry loop where I suspect the issue might be creeping in:

```cpp
void process_sensor_data(const SensorPacket& packet) {
    // Allocate buffer for processing
    uint8_t* raw_buffer = new uint8_t[packet.size()];
    memcpy(raw_buffer, packet.data, packet.size());

    auto result = DataProcessor::analyze(raw_buffer, packet.size());

    if (result.status == Status::ERROR) {
        Logger::log_error("Processing failed", result.code);
        // Potentially missing cleanup here?
    }

    // We use a singleton for the ring buffer
    RingBuffer::get_instance().push(result.processed_data);

    delete[] raw_buffer;
}
```

The heap looks fine for the first few hours, but then it just starts climbing. Can you give me a systematic approach to finding exactly where these bytes are disappearing?
